      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently reviewing the stability profiles for a new low-fat mayonnaise formulation. We're seeing some issues with phase separation in high-acid environments (pH < 3.8). Can you explain the specific molecular role of lecithin in stabilizing the oil-water interface under these high proton concentrations, and how the amphiphilic nature of the molecule helps prevent coalescence?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin acts as an effective surfactant in low-pH environments because its polar head group remains protonated while its hydrophobic tails retain strong affinity for the oil phase, maintaining a stable orientation at the interface. This amphiphilic structure forms a steric barrier around droplets that physically prevents them from merging (coalescing), even as high proton concentrations might otherwise disrupt weaker ionic emulsifiers. By creating a robust mechanical shield, lecithin minimizes interfacial tension and inhibits creaming, ensuring the stability of your low-fat mayonnaise formulation. Adjuncting this might involve carefully selecting egg yolk whey proteins or certain pH-tolerant cationic gums to further reinforce the emulsion network against acidic challenges.",
