      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The breakdown in low-pH environments often stems from a combination of charge screening rather than simple physical displacement, as high proton concentrations compress the electrical double layer around droplets. When the electrostatic repulsion is suppressed by these excess cations, van der Waals forces dominate, causing rapid Ostwald ripening where solvent molecules diffuse faster from smaller to larger droplets. This kinetics-driven process accelerates droplet growth and coalescence, leading to the mandatory separation you are observing in your formulation. To mitigate this, you might consider adding polyelectrolytes to reform charge or using non-ionic emulsifiers that are less sensitive to pH-induced screening effects.",
